   "profile": "J3",
   "kind": "notes",
   "texts": {
    "profile": "## Militarism\n- Historians often describe four long-term causes of the First World War and one event that set it off, and the first cause is militarism.\n- Before 1914 the great powers built large armies and navies, and Germany and Britain raced to build battleships.\n- Think of a video game upgrade contest as an analogy for the material's point that Germany and Britain raced to build battleships.\n- Generals planned for war, and many leaders came to see war as a normal way to settle disputes.\n\n## Alliances\n- The second cause is alliances, and Europe was divided into two blocs.\n- The Triple Entente joined France, Russia and Britain, and the Triple Alliance joined Germany, Austria-Hungary and Italy.\n- Because of these alliances, a local conflict could pull in many countries, and a quarrel between two states could grow into a continental war.\n- Think of a basketball team's defensive rotation as an analogy for the material's point that a local conflict could pull in many countries.\n\n## Imperialism\n- The third cause is imperialism, which means rivalry between empires for colonies, markets and power.\n- The European empires competed for land and resources in Africa and Asia, which increased distrust between them.\n\n## Nationalism\n- The fourth cause is nationalism, which means strong pride in one's nation or a wish for independence.\n- Think of a comic artist's pride in a finished page as an analogy for the material's definition of nationalism.\n- Nationalism was especially powerful in the Balkans, where Serbia wanted to unite the Slavic peoples and Austria-Hungary feared losing its empire.\n\n## The Spark and Alliance Chain\n- Step 1: Before this step, the material lists four long-term causes; the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand in Sarajevo on 28 June 1914 occurs as the spark.\n- Step 2: Before this step, Austria-Hungary's war on Serbia exists; because of the alliances, Russia, Germany, France and Britain are drawn in.\n- Check: The material confirms the result by stating that, because of the alliances, Austria-Hungary's war on Serbia soon drew in Russia, Germany, France and Britain.\n- Optional harder question: Why could a local conflict pull in many countries and grow into a continental war? The material answers that this happened because of these alliances.",
    "mirror": "## Four causes plus a spark\n- Historians usually name four long-term causes of the First World War, plus one event that set it off.\n- Layout: militarism → alliances → imperialism → nationalism → the spark, in that order.\n- You can hold the shape as four long-term pressures with one trigger event at the end.\n\n## Militarism\n- Militarism came first: in the years before 1914 the great powers built up large armies and navies.\n- the great powers built up large armies and navies\n- Germany and Britain raced each other to build battleships.\n- Generals began to plan for war.\n- Many leaders came to see war as a normal way to settle disputes.\n\n## Alliances\n- Alliances came second, and Europe was divided into two blocs.\n- Layout: Triple Entente = France, Russia, Britain | Triple Alliance = Germany, Austria-Hungary, Italy.\n- The Triple Entente joined France, Russia and Britain together.\n- The Triple Alliance joined Germany, Austria-Hungary and Italy together.\n- Because of these alliances, a local conflict could pull in many countries.\n- Think of it like a comic where two characters fight and the whole cast picks sides, so a small scrap becomes a story-wide brawl.\n- So a quarrel between two states could grow into a continental war.\n\n## Imperialism\n- Imperialism came third, and it means rivalry between empires for colonies, markets and power.\n- The European empires competed for land and resources in Africa and Asia.\n- That rivalry increased distrust between the empires.\n\n## Nationalism and the spark\n- Nationalism came fourth: it means strong pride in your nation, or a wish for independence.\n- Nationalism was especially powerful in the Balkans.\n- There Serbia wanted to unite the Slavic peoples, and Austria-Hungary feared losing its empire.\n- The spark was the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand, heir to the Austro-Hungarian throne, in Sarajevo on 28 June 1914.\n- Because of the alliances, Austria-Hungary's war on Serbia soon drew in Russia, Germany, France and Britain.\n- Think of it like a basketball play where one pass pulls every defender in — that's the alliances drawing in Russia, Germany, France and Britain."
